From c539b5ca123eaf3bb1ef654df569047a28dfba51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 16:17:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/hip: fold the MoE routing weight into the MMQ prefill
 epilogue (+2.3% prefill, byte-exact)

0034 folded the MoE routing-weight multiply into the mul_mat_id epilogue at the
decode shape, where MUL_MAT_ID takes mmvq. At prefill batches the same node
takes MMQ instead, so the multiply survives as a full elementwise pass over
[n_embd, n_expert_used, n_tokens]: on the pp512 census it is 40 launches of
87 us, 3.48 ms of a 114.9 ms pass, moving 33 MB of read-modify-write per layer
to apply one scalar per column - while the matmul that produced those bytes had
every one of them in a register.

The index needs no new bookkeeping. MMQ writes through
dst[ids_dst[j]*stride_col_dst + i], and for MUL_MAT_ID ids_dst[j] is the flat
destination row token*n_expert_used + slot - which is exactly the flat index
into the [1, n_expert_used, n_tokens] routing-weight vector. So the epilogue
multiplies by dst_scale[ids_dst[j]] and stores once.

Bit-exact by the same argument as 0034: the multiplicand is the same rounded f32
the stock kernel would have written and the MUL would have read back. Server
greedy is byte-identical to the fold-off control on 6/6 prompts.

The scaled store loop is a SEPARATE loop in both write_backs rather than a
conditional inside the stock one, and that is the whole performance story. The
first version put "if (dst_scale) val *= dst_scale[ids_dst[j]]" inside the
unrolled store loop, which reads as free when the pointer is null. Measured
against a same-source control built from the parent commit, it cost 1.3% of
pp512 on every MMQ launch in the build whether or not the fold fired (the Q5_K
expert-down went 456.6 -> 478.5 us with the fold off), and the patch as a whole
was a wash: 1.0013 against the control. With the two loops split the fold-off
arm is 0.9992 against the control - neutral - and the fold-on arm is 1.0228.
A conditional global load inside a fully unrolled store loop is not free.

Detection mirrors 0034 with the decode guard inverted: a (MUL_MAT_ID, MUL)
subgraph, an f32 factor of shape [1, n_expert_used, n_tokens] contiguous, an
identically laid out MUL destination, and the exact dispatch conditions under
which ggml_cuda_mul_mat_id would have taken the MMQ route (quantized src0, batch
above the mmvq mmid cutoff, ggml_cuda_should_use_mmq). Stream-k stays correct:
the partial written to the fixup buffer is left unscaled and the fixup kernel
applies the weight as it accumulates. GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MOE_WEIGHT_FUSE_MMQ=1
restores the stock pair.

Measured (runner box, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0, whole-process interleaved 3-arm
A/B against a control binary built from the parent commit):

- pp512  control 4277-4344 (median 4305.7), fold OFF 4268-4342 (median 4302.3,
  ratio 0.9992), fold ON 4368-4443 (median 4424.9, ratio 1.0277 vs control,
  per-round median 1.0228); arms disjoint 5/5, min(ON) > max(control)
- tg128 neutral in all three arms (145.4-145.9)
- pp512 kernel census: 2276 -> 2236 dispatches per pass, 117.4 -> 112.0 ms of
  kernel; the 40 k_bin_bcast op_mul launches at grid 1024x8x512 are gone
- gate ppl -c 512 --chunks 8: ON 3.9249 / 3.9269 (two loads), OFF 3.9271,
  stock 3.9314 -> -0.11 to -0.17%, in band
- decode-path ppl -b 512 -ub 1 --chunks 8: 3.9338, identical to the banked
  stack (the fold is gated off the decode shape by construction)
- llama-server greedy 6/6 BYTE-EXACT vs the fold-off control, 276-1194 B
- test-backend-ops MUL_MAT_ID / MUL_MAT / MUL OK
